# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# VERIFICATION TARGET  —  copy to .env.verify.local to isolate the verify scripts
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The scripts in scripts/verify-*.mjs plant fixtures and drive the real job
# runner. While .env.local pointed at a database nobody else touched, that was
# deterministic. It no longer is: Vercel Cron calls /api/jobs/run every sixty
# seconds against the production project, so a verification run and the live
# runner compete for the same queue.
#
# Creating this file points the scripts — and the dev server you run them
# against — at a database of their own. The scripts pick it up automatically
# and say so on the first line of their output.
#
# ── Option A: the local Supabase stack (needs Docker) ────────────────────────
#
#   npm run verify:db:up        # supabase start, then applies migrations + seed
#
# The stack prints its own API URL and keys. The API URL is fixed by
# supabase/config.toml; the keys are generated per machine, so copy them from
# the `supabase start` output rather than trusting the values below.
#
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:54321
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=<anon key printed by supabase start>
#   SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=<service_role key printed by supabase start>
#
# ── Option B: a second Supabase cloud project ────────────────────────────────
#
# Create an empty project, then `supabase link` to it and `npm run db:push` to
# apply supabase/migrations. Use its own URL and keys below. Do not point this
# at the production project — that is the situation this file exists to escape.
#
# ── Either way ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# CRON_SECRET may be anything at least 16 characters; it only has to match the
# value the dev server you are testing against was started with. It is NOT the
# production secret and should not be.
#
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL must point at a dev server started with THIS same file,
#   otherwise the scripts drive an app connected to a different database:
#
#     npm run verify:dev        # next dev with .env.verify.local
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

#   WHATSAPP_VERIFY_TOKEN and WHATSAPP_APP_SECRET are needed only by
#   `npm run db:verify:webhook`, which drives the real inbound webhook and has
#   to sign its deliveries the way Meta does. Like CRON_SECRET they only have to
#   match the dev server you are testing against, and they are NOT the
#   production values.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL=http://localhost:3000
CRON_SECRET=
WHATSAPP_VERIFY_TOKEN=
WHATSAPP_APP_SECRET=

#   ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL points the SDK at the stub server that
#   `npm run db:verify:proposal` starts on 127.0.0.1:54399, so extraction runs
#   end to end without a real model call and without a real key. The key must
#   be set — the provider does not register without one — but any string does.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=verify-stub-key-not-a-real-credential
ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:54399
